# Hand-authored conservative box: K_cube placement reachability (moving=Cube, support=LF).
# Local frame = support foot frame (X forward, Y lateral, Z up), rotated into world by the
# same foot_frame_rotation as every other reachability polytope. Explicitly a placeholder
# approximation, not derived from robot kinematics (docs/cube-implementation-plan.md).
#
# History, found by empirically validating against StairsGap rather than by construction:
#  1. First version had forward on local Y, lateral on local X -- wrong for a yaw=0 foot in
#     a corridor/staircase scenario (progress axis is world X): swapped, forward is X.
#  2. Centered on the support foot (Y centered on 0) put the cube inside the real LF-in-RF/
#     RF-in-LF reachability's own minimum ~0.19m lateral stance-width clearance: unreachable
#     by EITHER foot afterward. Offset Y past that clearance, toward the side the OTHER foot
#     (the one that will step onto it) naturally reaches: RF, y<0 for support=LF.
#  3. A wide forward reach (X up to 0.4-0.45) overshot the real per-step reachability's own
#     X range (roughly +/-0.33m): the stepping foot could never reach a cube placed further
#     forward than a normal step could anyway. Narrowed to a modest forward range.
#  4. On the real StairsGap scenario specifically, the floor surface's own (foot-eroded)
#     boundary sits close to the start position (~0.19m away) -- a forward range starting
#     too far out (e.g. [0.1,0.3]) put the WHOLE box past that edge, so no placement was
#     ever valid there regardless of K_cube's own reach. Narrowed further, starting at 0.
#
# c (cube base/contact point) reachable box: X in [0.0,0.15] (forward), Y in [-0.35,-0.2]
# (lateral, offset for stance clearance), Z in [-0.05,0.10] (vertical).
v 0.000000 -0.350000 -0.050000
v 0.000000 -0.350000 0.100000
v 0.000000 -0.200000 -0.050000
v 0.000000 -0.200000 0.100000
v 0.150000 -0.350000 -0.050000
v 0.150000 -0.350000 0.100000
v 0.150000 -0.200000 -0.050000
v 0.150000 -0.200000 0.100000
f 1 3 4 2
f 5 6 8 7
f 1 2 6 5
f 3 7 8 4
f 1 5 7 3
f 2 4 8 6
